  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "id": "H1",
      "text": "Paired rule-shift tasks distinguish memorization from re-reading and adaptation.",
      "status": "testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "H2",
      "text": "Provenance, trust, and expiry reduce stale and poisoned memory use compared with recency-only memory.",
      "status": "testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "H3",
      "text": "Transient tool-failure injection exposes recovery behavior that ordinary task success hides.",
      "status": "testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "H4",
      "text": "Deterministic negative controls catch a meaningful subset of invalid small-ML claims before a research agent reports success.",
      "status": "testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "H5",
      "text": "A balanced factorial task design can separate rule-shift and tool-fault effects and recover a constructed interaction using scripted reference policies.",
      "status": "harness-validated"
    },
    {
      "id": "H6",
      "text": "A three-factor task design can add a memory-poison condition whose effect is visible for naive memory and removed by provenance-aware memory.",
      "status": "harness-validated"
    },
    {
      "id": "H7",
      "text": "For complete balanced two-level designs, standard orthogonal factorial contrasts with nested family/episode bootstrap and Holm/BH adjustment recover planted effects with nominal finite-sample error behavior in the declared simulation.",
      "status": "harness-iterated"
    },
    {
      "id": "H8",
      "text": "A frozen 300-task CLEAR split across filesystem, mock-browser, and gridworld can be deterministically regenerated, automatically verified, and split into non-overlapping train/dev/test rule variants.",
      "status": "harness-validated"
    },
    {
      "id": "H9",
      "text": "Separating episodic, semantic, and procedural memory with provenance, trust, expiry, conflict detection, and active forgetting reduces poison selection compared with flat recency-only retrieval in the declared synthetic cross-session probe.",
      "status": "harness-validated"
    },
    {
      "id": "H10",
      "text": "In a deterministic four-fault recovery probe, replanning and state-check-replan controllers recover stale state, transient tool errors, misleading documentation, and rule changes without human intervention, whereas retry-only and rollback do not.",
      "status": "harness-validated"
    },
    {
      "id": "H11",
      "text": "An evidence-gated research-claim policy rejects more synthetic invalid findings than a naive improvement claim; a strict subgroup schema blocks malformed or undeclared positive claims, while missing declarations remain non-evaluable rather than detected.",
      "status": "harness-iterated"
    },
    {
      "id": "H12",
      "text": "A logistic monitor trained on observable synthetic goal-drift state features can rank held-out drift risk, but the current construction does not establish pre-drift warning or checkpoint-recovery benefit.",
      "status": "inconclusive"
    },
    {
      "id": "H13",
      "text": "Symbolic ensemble uncertainty can separate unseen states in a table world, but in v0 it does not rank OOD one-step errors or improve the tested planning objective.",
      "status": "inconclusive"
    },
    {
      "id": "H14",
      "text": "In a synthetic tabular verifier protocol, ensemble-disagreement reward improves OOD transfer and reduces transfer drop relative to answer reward, but no reward mode dominates success, calibration, hacking, and cost together.",
      "status": "harness-validated"
    },
    {
      "id": "H15",
      "text": "A contextual bandit over retrieve/reflect/tool/write-memory/stop can produce a stable action mix on CLEAR-300 contexts, but the v0 controller does not beat fixed reflect and does not learn to write memory.",
      "status": "inconclusive"
    }
  ],
